(project tuna-forensics
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: divergence forensics - the debuggability
      research question made falsifiable. The 2026-10-02 design
      review hypothesis: sabra code with the stdlib vocabulary is
      EXTREMELY debuggable relative to conventional stacks - at
      least for agent debuggers, whose surface is diffable
      structure, not prose. This chapter converts the adjective into
      a protocol over existing artifacts (sealed records,
      first-diff, journal forks, patch CAS, replay verification), a
      deliberate-fault corpus, and numeric gates the book can pass
      or fail in public. Sequenced after stdlib v1: the fault
      corpus injects into stdlib-bound programs, honoring the
      vocabulary-first dogfood gate.")
 (status planned)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section forensics-v1
  (doc "The protocol is a LOOP over existing API objects with zero
        ephemeral state: every fact in a debugging session is a
        hash-addressed artifact, so a forensics transcript replays
        in another agent's hands from nothing but the starting
        hashes. No new kernel code is in scope (core byte-identical
        rule as stdlib); implementation lands as scripts plus one
        verify number.")
  (status planned)
  (subsection protocol
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      SEAL. The failing run rides its deriv record: pinned program
      and input bytes, hash-chained journal, result hash, exact
      step count. Investigation starts from a portable object,
      never a live session.
      DIFF. first-diff over the known-good and failing program
      trees (and over the two records' output trees) walks to the
      first divergent path; the answer carries the path plus both
      subtree hashes.
      CAUSE. World-answered or code-answered? A journal fork
      re-executes with an edited prim answer: if the outcome moves,
      the divergence is journal-shaped; if not, it lives in the
      trees. Trace summaries add the per-firing digest view, and a
      divergent run's Loop closure already names the offending
      (fun, arg) pair - the infinite loop that names itself.
      LOCATE. Tree path -> callsite pair (program hash + path,
      AGENTS rule 8 law) -> provenance -> IR node + source span.
      Every step of the localization chain names hash-addressed
      facts, nothing else.
      FIX. Structural patch at the divergent path with CAS
      (expected old-subtree hash = the diff's failing subtree),
      producing a fresh program hash; old rows stay history.
      PROVE. Replay-verify the fix against the same recorded
      journal (world frozen), then a fresh live run where the fault
      class calls for one; record status and the new count beside
      the fault table.
      HANDOFF. Every query and cited artifact in the transcript is
      itself re-runnable; two agents handed the same starting
      hashes must reach the same localization, deterministically.
      That is the collaboration claim - debugging without shared
      state. |))))
  (subsection fault-corpus
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      N >= 12 deliberate faults over stdlib-v1-bound programs, four
      classes:
      CLASS 1 STRUCTURAL - a flipped ternary digit or a swapped arm
      in a pinned subtree; the mutation path is the expected
      answer; first-diff should land exactly on it.
      CLASS 2 SEMANTIC - a wrong dictionary def consumed by an
      unchanged caller; the program-hash first-diff lands at the
      def boundary; provenance names the def's IR span.
      CLASS 3 WORLD-VIA-JOURNAL - the prim answer in a forkable
      recorded run is wrong, never the program; the trees are
      identical, so only the fork protocol can localize it. This
      class exists to catch the siren call of tree-only debugging.
      CLASS 4 DIVERGENCE - an omega-shaped fault; the expected
      protocol answer is the Loop closure pair, never a fuel burn.
      Corpus files live in scripts/forensics-corpus/ with per-fault
      expected-path annotations; faults are produced by scripted
      mutation (never hand-edited), so the corpus is itself
      reproducible, and every injected mutation lands as a
      journaled CAS patch - the corpus is auditable history like
      everything else. |))))
  (subsection measurable-claims
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Gates the chapter can pass or fail in public. Numbers stay
      owner-mutable until make freezes them; after freeze, a miss
      is a FINDING, never a quietly relabeled test.
      1. LOCALIZATION. >= 90% of class-1 faults localized to the
         exact mutation path by first-diff alone; >= 80% of
         class-2 faults to the mutated def's boundary.
      2. QUERY BUDGET. A single class-1/2 fault is fully localized
         (record -> callsite path + IR span) within <= 8 API
         queries; a class-3 fault within <= 12 including the fork.
      3. COLLABORATION. Two protocol runs from identical starting
         hashes agree on every localization answer exactly;
         transcript queries are part of the compared state.
      4. COUNTERFACTUAL. Every class-3 fault's fork reaches a
         different terminal status, and replay-verify passes both
         records.
      5. UNATTENDED. The full loop seal->handoff runs as
         scripts/verify-9-forensics.sh against the live server
         with zero human state; exit 0 green, 1 any-miss, 2
         malformed, mirroring deriv-check exit semantics.
      CLAIMS DELIBERATELY NOT MADE (recorded so future agents do
      not overreach; the review briefing line 'count preservation
      proves behavior preservation' was wrong and dies here): step
      count is NOT a behavior-equivalence oracle across different
      tree shapes - a constant swap changes behavior and preserves
      the count (counterexample pinned in the corpus). The count
      law pins identical program + inputs across engines, and is a
      workload signal (broken base cases flame up as count or fuel
      deltas) plus a record-integrity pin (deriv equality) - which
      is all this corpus claims through it. |))))
  (subsection acceptance
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      1. The corpus lands with scripted mutation sources plus
         expected annotations; every injection is a journaled CAS
         patch.
      2. scripts/verify-9-forensics.sh reports the measured number
         beside each gate; a miss is recorded in FINDINGS.md under
         the failure definition below, never an unpassed test kept
         adjacent-undocumented.
      3. CORE UNTOUCHED: interpreter/, compiler/, common/ stay
         byte-identical; delta confined to scripts/ (server/cli
         only if a finding legitimately forces protocol surface).
      4. SEQUENCING GATE: make follows stdlib v1's acceptance -
         faults ride stdlib programs, so this corpus says nothing
         until the vocabulary exists.
      5. borge lint/report green; the full battery green. |))))
  (subsection pre-registered-failure
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      F7 SUPERLATIVE OVERREACH. TRIGGER: any frozen gate 1-5
      misses. THEN: the operator pitch language drops the word
      EXTREMELY and retains 'a debugging discipline over
      hash-addressed artifacts - receipts all the way down'; the
      artifacts stay load-bearing (records remain receipts, forks
      remain counterfactuals), and the superlative dies in
      FINDINGS.md, not in marketing - same regime F5/F6. The
      inverse overreach is also pre-failed: gates passing scopes
      the claim to stdlib-v1-scale programs and agent debuggers
      only; any blast radius out to 'all agent work' is a future
      chapter with its own corpus, never this one's marketing. |))))))
